- retained Mattermost's dedicated per-model stores and reusable conformance
  tests while adapting them to Proctor's split credential schema;
- replaced product-specific API handler state with an immutable, minimal
  request principal and explicit per-route authentication classifications;
- retained bearer credentials for CLI sessions and added Mattermost-inspired
  host-only Electron/web cookies, with Proctor-specific split access/refresh
  paths, rotating signed double-submit CSRF verification, rejection of mixed
  or duplicate credential sources, and no credentials in URL parameters;
- adapted active-session listing and self-service individual/account-wide
  revocation while retaining ownership checks and sanitized responses;
- added a Proctor-specific per-user PostgreSQL advisory-lock protocol shared by
  login, refresh rotation, individual revocation, and revoke-all so concurrent
  credentials cannot escape an account security reset;
- adapted Mattermost's purpose-token and account-email flows into
  target-bound, hashed, expiring credentials with atomic replacement and
  single-use consumption;
- made email verification and password reset transactional with their account
  mutation and terminal security audit, and made password reset revoke every
  active session in the same transaction;
- added generic public reset responses, shared-cache request/completion
  throttling, fragment-delivered browser links, in-memory mail integration
  tests, and explicit strong/recent session wrappers;
- added finite, explicitly scoped personal access tokens with hashed
  persistence, one-time credential delivery, optional academic-unit ceilings,
  recent-session creation and re-enablement, reversible disablement, debounced
  usage metadata, durable audits, and a separate ordinary-principal route class
  that does not weaken interactive session boundaries.
- retained Mattermost's TOTP setup, activation, verification-window, and
  replay-prevention behavior while storing the secret in a separate
  AES-256-GCM encrypted aggregate and storing recovery-code hashes separately;
- implemented the RFC 6238 code calculation with Go's standard cryptography
  packages rather than taking a dependency on Mattermost's unlicensed TOTP
  helper module;
- coupled MFA activation, challenge, disablement, recovery-code replacement,
  and session-assurance changes to durable audits, transactional replay
  prevention, and immediate access-cache invalidation, while enforcing the
  same replay-protected second-factor aggregate during login;
- adapted administrative session listing and revocation around Proctor's
  dedicated `session.view` and `session.manage` actions and user-resource
  authorization.

